# Upstream's own ignore conventions: this release ships none.
#
# The FreeType 2.14.3 tarball carries no `.gitignore` at any depth, so there is
# no upstream entry here to preserve, re-include or make room for, and nothing
# below removes one. Upstream's ignore rules live in its development repository,
# which is not a source for this tree.
#
# What follows is the fork's own list. The first block is the build output
# upstream's own build systems would leave in this tree if a contributor ran
# them here; the entries are named after artefacts, not after a guess.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# See FORK-LICENSE.md for what that identifier covers.

# Upstream's object directory, named at `Makefile:29`
# (`OBJ_DIR ?= $(TOP_DIR)/objs`). It is the one directory the release carries and
# this tree does not, and `build.zig.zon`'s `.paths` does not name it either.
objs/

# Object, archive, shared-object and import-library output. `MSBuild.rsp` and
# `MSBuild.sln` are in the tree, so a Visual Studio or MinGW build here would
# produce these beside the sources.
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.so.*
*.obj
*.lib
*.exp
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.dll
*.exe
*.manifest

# zig build artefacts (fork addition).
#
# These are directories rather than single files because a normal `zig build`
# creates them, and a normal `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into
# the package published from it. None of them is named in build.zig.zon
# `.paths`, so an ignored directory can never become part of the package.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/
# Zig 0.16 materialises a fetched dependency into a project-local directory.
# This package declares no dependencies, so this exists only so that a future
# one cannot commit a fetched tree.
zig-pkg/
# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. The entries carry the `consumer/` prefix rather than living in a
# file of their own, so a build run from the repository root cannot commit them
# by accident and so this package has no second ignore file to keep in step.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts (fork addition).
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild)
# into the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved
# inputs and its state beside the project. None of them is named in
# build.zig.zon `.paths`, so an ignored path can never become part of the
# package either. `devenv.lock` and `flake.lock` are deliberately *not* here:
# they are real inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be
# the wrong way to keep a build out of a commit.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/
